<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merano</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Meran</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Merano is a spa town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the Passer near its junction with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adige">Adige</a>. Lying below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol-castle">Tyrol Castle</a>, it was the chief town of the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> until the princes moved their residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> in the 15th century. It became a fashionable health resort in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merano.jpg" alt="Merano" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rolf Kranz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meran,_Panorama_(Blick_vom_Pulverturm).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Merano, in German Meran, is a municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-tyrol">South Tyrol</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the second-largest city of South Tyrol after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a>. It lies in a basin at the entrance to the Passeier Valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vinschgau">Vinschgau</a>, surrounded by mountains that rise to 3,335 metres. Both the Italian and German names are official, and an early form, Mairania, is recorded from 857.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited since the third millennium BCE, and the history of the town proper begins in 15 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded a road station there, Statio Maiensis. In the 13th century the Counts at Castle Tyrol raised it to the status of a city and made it capital of their County of Tyrol. The county passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a> in 1363, and in 1420 Duke Friedrich IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>. Merano stayed the official capital until 1848 but lost almost all its importance as a trading point on the roads between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>In 1809 a peasants' army won a victory over French and Bavarian forces on the Küchelberg above the city. In the 19th century its mild Alpine climate made Meran a spa town within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-kafka">Franz Kafka</a> and Empress Elisabeth. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, under the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye, it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Merano combines Austrian and Italian cultural heritage. Its medieval city gates survive, as do the Gothic St Nicholas' Church and the Princely Castle, which was a residence of Archduke Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. The spa years left the Kurhaus, the Stadttheater, the Empress Elisabeth Park and arched promenades along the Passer. Tirol Castle stands close by, and vineyards extend into the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Merano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts of Tyrol, named after Tyrol Castle near <b>Merano</b>, appear around 1140 as advocates of the bishoprics of Brixen and Trent.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li></ul></section>
